Step 4: Integrating OTP authentication into login and register workflows
Update the login controller:
public function store(LoginRequest $request): RedirectResponse
{
    $request->authenticate();
    $request->session()->regenerate();
    return redirect()->intended(route('request', absolute: false));
}
Update the registration controller:
public function store(Request $request): RedirectResponse
{
    $request->validate([
        'name' => 'required|string|max:255',
        'email' => 'required|string|lowercase|email|max:255|unique:' . User::class,
        'password' => ['required', 'confirmed', Rules\Password::defaults()],
    ]);
    $user = User::create([
        'name' => $request->name,
        'email' => $request->email,
        'password' => Hash::make($request->password),
    ]);
    event(new Registered($user));
    Auth::login($user);
    return redirect(route('request', absolute: false));
}
Conclusion
Implementing OTP authentication in Laravel and Vue.js enhances security for user logins and transactions. By generating, sending, and verifying OTPs, we can add an extra layer of protection against unauthorized access. This method is particularly useful for financial applications and sensitive user data.

SMS is a good secondary channel for game account signup verification links, and a Node.js worker can turn those event notification alerts into observable delivery states. Send once, poll status, and expose sent, delivered, failed, or undeliverable in the signup UI. Retry only recoverable failures. Keep verification email as the primary path rather than treating SMS delivery as proof that a person completed verification.
TL;DR: model the notification as a small state machine, not a fire-and-forget API call. Put a per-user cooldown, a country allowlist, and a spend threshold in front of the provider. Preserve your own signup ID and policy decision because provider-side tag aggregation is not a substitute for compliance evidence.
Replace send-and-forget with an evidence trail
The weak mental model is short: signup happens, the backend sends a text, and a 200 means success. It does not. A successful send request establishes acceptance by the API; the later delivery state is separate.
Acceptance is not delivery.
The useful mental model has five steps. Picture it as a line: policy decision -> accepted send -> status polling -> user-visible state -> audit record. Each arrow should leave a timestamped record tied to one internal signup ID. That record answers the questions a compliance reviewer will ask later: which country rule allowed the attempt, when was it accepted, what terminal delivery state was observed, and why was a resend allowed?
This matters for fitness class waitlists too. The wording and urgency differ, but the mechanism does not: an opening can expire while a message is in flight, so the product must distinguish accepted, delivered, and failed instead of showing one vague "sent" badge.
Infrai is one reasonable consolidation choice when the same backend also uses other services: one key and one bill reduce credential sprawl and month-end invoice reconciliation. Its SMS surface supports one-off sending plus status and event polling, while its public discovery surface describes capability schemas. The trade-off is important: there are no webhook event pushes in these communication namespaces, so the application must poll and will not get webhook-speed orchestration.
How should Node.js poll SMS event notification delivery alerts?
The code below deliberately sits before any provider call. It is runnable TypeScript and covers the controls that SMS providers cannot infer from your product rules: allowed destinations, user cooldown, and a country-level spend circuit breaker. It also emits a compact decision record that can be stored beside the signup attempt.
type Country = "US" | "GB" | "DE" | "FR";
type Attempt = {
  signupId: string;
  userId: string;
  country: string;
  lastSentAt?: number;
  countrySpendUsd: number;
};
type Decision =
  | { allowed: true; audit: Record<string, string | number> }
  | { allowed: false; reason: string; audit: Record<string, string | number> };
const allowedCountries = new Set<Country>(["US", "GB", "DE", "FR"]);
const cooldownMs = 60_000;
const spendLimitUsd = 25;
const apiKey = process.env.INFRAI_API_KEY;
const apiBaseUrl = `https://${["api", "infrai", "cc"].join(".")}/v1`;
if (!apiKey) throw new Error("INFRAI_API_KEY is required");
export function authorizeSms(attempt: Attempt, now = Date.now()): Decision {
  const audit = {
    signupId: attempt.signupId,
    userId: attempt.userId,
    country: attempt.country,
    evaluatedAt: new Date(now).toISOString(),
    policyVersion: "signup-sms-v1",
  };
  if (!allowedCountries.has(attempt.country as Country)) {
    return { allowed: false, reason: "country_not_allowed", audit };
  }
  if (attempt.countrySpendUsd >= spendLimitUsd) {
    return { allowed: false, reason: "country_spend_limit", audit };
  }
  if (attempt.lastSentAt && now - attempt.lastSentAt < cooldownMs) {
    return { allowed: false, reason: "user_cooldown", audit };
  }
  return { allowed: true, audit };
}
const sleep = (milliseconds: number) =>
  new Promise<void>((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, milliseconds));
async function pollSmsStatus(messageId: string): Promise<unknown> {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 6; attempt += 1) {
    const response = await fetch(
      `${apiBaseUrl}/sms/status/${encodeURIComponent(messageId)}`,
      {
        method: "GET",
        headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${apiKey}` },
      },
    );
    if (response.status === 429) {
      const retryAfter = Number(response.headers.get("retry-after"));
      const delayMs = Number.isFinite(retryAfter)
        ? retryAfter * 1_000
        : 500 * 2 ** attempt;
      await sleep(delayMs);
      continue;
    }
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Status poll failed (${response.status}): ${await response.text()}`);
    }
    return response.json() as Promise<unknown>;
  }
  throw new Error("Status poll remained rate-limited after 6 attempts");
}
const decision = authorizeSms({
  signupId: "signup_7f3c",
  userId: "user_2048",
  country: "DE",
  countrySpendUsd: 12.4,
});
if (!decision.allowed) throw new Error(decision.reason);
const messageId = process.argv[2];
if (!messageId) throw new Error("Pass the provider message ID as the first argument");
console.log(JSON.stringify({ decision, status: await pollSmsStatus(messageId) }));
Those numbers are example application policy, not provider limits. Pick them from fraud tolerance, messaging consent, expected signup volume, and finance controls. Version the policy. A bare log line saying "blocked" is much less useful six months later than a structured record saying country_spend_limit under signup-sms-v1. The example accepts an existing message ID because the verified send request schema is not reproduced here; inventing a convenient to or body field would make the snippet look complete while teaching an unverified contract.
After authorization, call the provider's send operation with an idempotency key derived from the signup and notification purpose. For Infrai, authentication uses Authorization: Bearer <key>, and the key belongs in an environment variable. Poll the documented status or event operation with bounded exponential backoff, honor Retry-After on HTTP 429, check every response status, and stop after a terminal state or a product-defined deadline. There is no value in a tight loop. It creates load without making carrier delivery move faster.
Six attempts are visible in the example. That is a tutorial bound, not a service guarantee.
Keep the copy deterministic: one purpose, one expiring verification link, no optional prose assembled at runtime. Store business metadata in your database. Infrai does not expose cost reporting aggregated by tag through the API, so an internal signup ID, country, policy version, provider message ID, and observed states form the durable join.
Which provider fits the control model?
There is no universally best choice. Compare the operational contract you are prepared to own.
| Option | Useful fit | Boundary to verify before committing | 
|---|---|---|
| Twilio Programmable Messaging | Teams that want a dedicated communications platform and documented messaging workflows | Confirm current country rules, sender requirements, status behavior, and pricing for every destination | 
| Vonage SMS API | Teams comparing a dedicated global SMS API with another carrier-facing option | Validate regional availability, compliance requirements, callbacks or polling, and retry semantics | 
| Amazon SNS | AWS-centered systems that prefer messaging inside existing cloud access and billing controls | Check origination identities, regional SMS settings, delivery reporting, and country-specific constraints | 
| Infrai | Backends that value one REST API, one key, and one bill across multiple service categories | Communication events are pull-based; app-owned geo fencing, spend breakers, metadata, and polling remain required | 
That comparison is intentionally not a price table. Rates, carrier fees, and country rules move. A useful proof of concept sends to the actual destination mix, records terminal-state latency, exercises a 429, and demonstrates that a repeated signup action cannot create duplicate sends.
The sharp trade-off is polling latency versus request volume: shorter intervals surface state sooner but consume more calls, while longer intervals leave the UI stale. Choose the interval from the product deadline, then measure backlog age and call volume together. Do not tune either metric alone.
The decision rule is crisp: choose a dedicated platform when communications-specific tooling and channel depth dominate; choose a cloud-native option when existing cloud governance is the main constraint; consider a consolidated API when reducing keys, integrations, and invoices is worth owning the polling loop. Infrai has no voice, WhatsApp, or RCS channel in this surface, so it is the wrong basis for a roadmap that requires those channels.
When should a failed notification be resent or canceled?
Resend is for a recoverable failure after the latest observed status justifies another attempt. It is not a second send button for an impatient client. The backend should enforce the cooldown, acquire an idempotent operation lock, re-check whether the verification has already completed, and then record the reason for the retry.
Cancel has a narrower meaning. Use it only for a pending scheduled SMS flow when the product allows the user to stop the alert. A message already handed to downstream delivery cannot be treated as recalled merely because the UI changed. For signup verification, expiration and server-side token invalidation are the real security controls.
One trap is easy to miss: email scheduling and SMS scheduling do not have identical cancellation capabilities in this API surface. Do not build a generic cancelNotification() abstraction that promises symmetry the underlying channels do not have. Make the state machine admit channel-specific transitions.
That asymmetry is real.
What about replies, consent, and regional compliance?
If the product accepts inbound replies for STOP or help workflows, poll inbound messages and immediately project opt-outs into suppression logic. Polling means there is an inherent delay, so set a conservative interval and prevent new sends while an opt-out update is being reconciled. Provider suppression tools can help, but the product still needs a single authoritative consent state.
Country allowlists are only the first gate. They do not prove legal compliance. Sender identity, consent, quiet hours, message content, data retention, and recordkeeping can vary by destination and use case; verify them against the provider's current country guidance and counsel appropriate to the deployment. In particular, a pending domestic email vendor cannot serve as evidence for compliance in China.
Email fallback also needs honest boundaries. There is no managed email OTP operation here, so an email verification-code fallback requires application-owned code generation, storage, expiry, attempt limits, and consumption. There is no SMTP relay. A verification link can still be sent through an email API, but do not describe that as managed OTP.
Observe four things: policy denials by reason, accepted sends, state-transition counts, and age of the oldest nonterminal message. Alert on ratios and backlog age, not on every individual failure. The first gives an operator a system signal; the second creates noise during ordinary carrier variance.
The final architecture is pleasantly small. The application decides. The provider transports. The polling worker observes. The database proves what happened.

A success message after a form submit usually looks like one function call. On the shadcn toast page it is toast.add with a title, and the Toaster has to sit in your layout before any message shows.
What the shadcn toast page covers
The page describes the component as "A succinct message that is displayed temporarily." The install command is pnpm dlx shadcn@latest add toast. The setup has two parts. A Toaster goes once in app/layout.tsx, and a toast function is imported from the same @/components/ui/toast path wherever you need a message.
The usage example calls toast.add with a title and a description. An action, such as an undo button, goes in an actionProps object, and toast.close takes the id that toast.add returns. The page also has a Types section that describes a type option and a Promise demo. Both of their code blocks are collapsed on the page, so check the View Code panel before you rely on either one.
The page does not say Toast is deprecated, does not mention Sonner, and does not recommend another library. It has no sentence about when to use a toast. The rules below are ours, and they are labelled as ours.
Mount the Toaster once
The Toaster renders the stack of messages. Put it in the root layout, inside the body and after your page content, so every route can show a message. Mounting it in a single page means a message fired from another page will not appear.
import { Toaster } from "@/components/ui/toast"
export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode
}) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>
        <main>{children}</main>
        <Toaster />
      </body>
    </html>
  )
}
Show success or error after a submit
The common case is a form that either saves or fails. Call toast.add in both branches, so the user always gets a result. The success message states what happened. The error message states what failed and gives the user something to do next.
"use client"
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button"
import { toast } from "@/components/ui/toast"
export function ProfileForm() {
  async function onSave() {
    try {
      await saveProfile() // your save call
      toast.add({
        title: "Profile saved",
        description: "Your changes are live.",
      })
    } catch {
      toast.add({
        title: "Could not save",
        description: "Check your connection and try again.",
      })
    }
  }
  return <Button onClick={onSave}>Save profile</Button>
}
Keep the form's input when the save fails. A message that says "could not save" while the form clears has taken the user's work with it. Clear the form only after the success branch runs.
Offer an undo
Some actions can be reversed, such as archiving a project. The toast can carry a single action. The page's example passes an actionProps object with the button label as children and an onClick handler. Keep the id that toast.add returns, and close the toast from inside the handler after the reversal:
const id = toast.add({
  title: "Project archived",
  actionProps: {
    children: "Undo",
    onClick() {
      restoreProject() // your restore call
      toast.close(id)
    },
  },
})
Only offer an action the user can actually reverse. If the action cannot be undone, put the warning in a dialog before the change, not in a toast after it.
Write the wording
Our rule for the copy is short. The title states the result in a few words. The description states the next step, or says nothing when the title is enough. Avoid vague titles such as "Done" or "Error", because the user cannot tell what changed.
Length matters more than most teams expect. A title of a few words reads in one glance. A title of twenty words wraps on a phone and pushes the description below the fold. Keep titles short and move detail into the description. If a message needs three sentences, it is probably a dialog or a line on the page, not a toast.
Say what happened in plain words. "Profile saved" states a result. "Success!" adds nothing, and the exclamation mark makes a routine save sound like an event. Avoid stacking the same error on every retry. Let the user act on the first message before you add another one.
Before you use the type option for success and error styling, read its code on the page. The code is collapsed, so the example you can see does not show it. Until you have checked it, the title and description carry the meaning on their own.
Test before you ship
Toasts fail where the network, the screen size and the keyboard meet. Check these before you ship:
- Turn the network off and submit. The error toast should show, and the form should keep the user's input.
- Submit twice quickly. The success toast should show once for one save, and the button should not allow a second submit while the first is running.
- Submit several times in a row. Toasts stack, so check that the stack does not cover the submit button on a 390px phone.
- Tab to the undo action after the toast appears. It should take focus, and pressing Enter should run the reversal.
- Wait for the toast to leave. Anything the user needs later should be on the page, not only in the toast, because the toast is temporary by design.
Run the phone check last, after the wording is final. Long titles wrap, and a wrapped message can push the stack over the page.
Where OTF stands
If you want a toast or a notification component already built into a product, we cannot point you to one. We checked the SaaS Dashboard, Booking, Fitness and Arcade kit pages for toast, notification and alert terms. None of them names a toast component. The SaaS Dashboard page lists "Inbox & notifications" as a feature. That is a feature of the product, not a toast component, and this article does not claim it is one.
The pricing page lists the Full-stack kits at $99 each and the Everything Bundle at $149, both as one-time payments, and it offers a live demo before you buy. Check each kit's repository for the components it ships before you rely on any of them. For the forms that use these messages, see the dialog and sheet guide, and for the table actions that often need a result message, see the data table guide. If you are choosing a starting point for a product, the pricing page is the place to compare kits side by side: OTF pricing.
Sources
- shadcn/ui, Toast: https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/components/toast
- OTF pricing: otf-kit.dev/pricing

The most annoying agent failure is not a bad edit. It is the agent announcing "all done" while the type checker is still failing.
Claude Code has a Stop hook that runs when the agent is about to finish its turn. If the hook exits with status 2, the stop is blocked and whatever the hook printed to stderr goes back to the agent as the reason. That gives you a cheap "you are not done yet" gate.
The hook
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import json, subprocess, sys
event = json.load(sys.stdin)
# Avoid an endless loop: if we already blocked once this turn, let it stop.
if event.get("stop_hook_active"):
    sys.exit(0)
check = subprocess.run(
    ["npx", "tsc", "--noEmit", "--pretty", "false"],
    capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=180,
)
if check.returncode != 0:
    print("Type check still fails. Fix these before finishing:\n" + check.stdout[-1500:], file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(2)
Wire it up
In .claude/settings.json:
{
  "hooks": {
    "Stop": [
      { "hooks": [ { "type": "command", "command": "python3 .claude/hooks/stop_gate.py" } ] }
    ]
  }
}
Notes from using it
- Keep the check fast. A full test suite on every stop gets old; a type check or a lint pass is usually enough.
- The stop_hook_active guard matters. Without it, a check the agent cannot fix will keep it looping.
- Pair it with a PostToolUse hook for per-edit checks and a PreToolUse deny list for dangerous commands, and you get three small, boring guardrails instead of one long prompt.
I keep this hook, the pre-edit deny list, and a short house-rules file together in a small Claude Code ops pack: Vildanden Agent Config. The free sample is enough to try the pattern.

The Go Blog
//go:fix inline and the source-level inliner
Go 1.26 contains an all-new implementation of the go fix subcommand,
designed to help you keep your Go code up-to-date and modern. For an
introduction, start by reading our recent post on the topic.
In this post, we’ll look at one particular feature, the source-level
inliner.
While go fix has several bespoke modernizers for specific new
language and library features,
the source-level inliner is the first fruit of our efforts to provide
“self-service” modernizers and analyzers.
It enables any package author to express simple API migrations and
updates in a straightforward and safe way.
We’ll first explain what the source-level inliner is and how you can use it,
then we’ll dive into some aspects of the problem and the technology behind it.
Source-level inlining
In 2023, we built an algorithm for source-level inlining of function calls in Go. To “inline” a call means to replace the call by a copy of the body of the called function, substituting arguments for parameters. We call it “source-level” inlining because it durably modifies the source code. By contrast, the inlining algorithm found in a typical compiler, including Go’s, applies a similar transformation, but to the compiler’s ephemeral intermediate representation, to generate more efficient code.
If you’ve ever invoked gopls’ “Inline call” interactive refactoring, you’ve used the source-level inliner. (In VS Code, this code action can be found on the “Source Action…” menu.) The before-and-after screenshots below show the effect of inlining the call to sum from the function named six.
The inliner is a crucial building block for a number of source transformation tools. For example, gopls uses it for the “Change signature” and “Remove unused parameter” refactorings because, as we’ll see below, it takes care of many subtle correctness issues that arise when refactoring function calls.
This same inliner is also one of the analyzers in the all-new go fix command.
In go fix, it enables self-service API migration and upgrades using a new //go:fix inline directive comment.
Let’s take a look at a few examples of how this works and what it can be used for.
Example: renaming ioutil.ReadFile
In Go 1.16, the ioutil.ReadFile function, which reads the content of a file, was deprecated in favor of the new os.ReadFile function. In effect, the function was renamed, though of course Go’s compatibility promise prevents us from ever removing the old name.
package ioutil
import "os"
// ReadFile reads the file named by filename…
// Deprecated: As of Go 1.16, this function simply calls [os.ReadFile].
func ReadFile(filename string) ([]byte, error) {
    return os.ReadFile(filename)
}
Ideally, we would like to change every Go program in the world to stop using ioutil.ReadFile and to call os.ReadFile instead. The inliner can help us do that. First we annotate the old function with //go:fix inline. This comment tells the tool that any time it sees a call to this function, it should inline the call.
package ioutil
import "os"
// ReadFile reads the file named by filename…
// Deprecated: As of Go 1.16, this function simply calls [os.ReadFile].
//go:fix inline
func ReadFile(filename string) ([]byte, error) {
    return os.ReadFile(filename)
}
When we run go fix on a file containing a call to ioutil.ReadFile, it applies the replacement:
$ go fix -diff ./...
-import "io/ioutil"
+import "os"
-   data, err := ioutil.ReadFile("hello.txt")
+   data, err := os.ReadFile("hello.txt")
The call has been inlined, in effect replacing a call to one function by a call to another.
Because the inliner replaces a function call by a copy of the body of
the called function, not by some arbitrary expression, in principle
the transformation should not change the program’s behavior
(barring code that inspects the call stack, of course).
This differs from other tools that allow for arbitrary rewrites,
such as gofmt -r, which are very powerful but need to be watched closely.
For many years now, our Google colleagues on the teams supporting Java, Kotlin, and C++ have been using source-level inliner tools like this. To date, these tools have eliminated millions of calls to deprecated functions in Google’s code base. Users simply add the directives, and wait. During the night, robots quietly prepare, test, and submit batches of code changes across a monorepo of billions of lines of code. If all goes well, by the morning the old code is no longer in use and can be safely deleted. Go’s inliner is a relative newcomer, but it has already been used to prepare more than 18,000 changelists to Google’s monorepo.
Example: fixing API design flaws
With a little creativity, a variety of migrations can be expressed as inlinings.
Consider this hypothetical oldmath package:
// Package oldmath is the bad old math package.
package oldmath
// Sub returns x - y.
func Sub(y, x int) int
// Inf returns positive infinity.
func Inf() float64
// Neg returns -x.
func Neg(x int) int
It has several design flaws: the Sub function declares its parameters in the wrong order; the Inf function implicitly prefers one of the two infinities; and the Neg function is redundant with Sub. Fortunately we have a newmath package that avoids these mistakes, and we’d like to get users to switch to it. The first step is to implement the old API in terms of the new package and to deprecate the old functions. Then we add inliner directives:
// Package oldmath is the bad old math package.
package oldmath
import "newmath"
// Sub returns x - y.
// Deprecated: the parameter order is confusing.
//go:fix inline
func Sub(y, x int) int {
    return newmath.Sub(x, y)
}
// Inf returns positive infinity.
// Deprecated: there are two infinite values; be explicit.
//go:fix inline
func Inf() float64 {
    return newmath.Inf(+1)
}
// Neg returns -x.
// Deprecated: this function is unnecessary.
//go:fix inline
func Neg(x int) int {
    return newmath.Sub(0, x)
}
Now, when users of oldmath run the go fix command on their code, it will replace all calls to the old functions by their new counterparts. By the way, gopls has included inline in its analyzer suite for some time, so if your editor uses gopls, the moment you add the //go:fix inline directives you should start seeing a diagnostic at each call site, such as “call of oldmath.Sub should be inlined”, along with a suggested fix that inlines that particular call.
For example, this old code:
import "oldmath"
var nine = oldmath.Sub(1, 10) // diagnostic: "call to oldmath.Sub should be inlined"
will be transformed to:
import "newmath"
var nine = newmath.Sub(10, 1)
Observe that after the fix, the arguments to Sub are in the logical order. This is progress! If you’re in luck, the inliner will succeed at removing every call to the functions in oldmath, perhaps allowing you to delete it as a dependency.
The inline analyzer works on types and constants too. If our oldmath package had originally declared a data type for rational numbers and a constant for π, we could use the following forwarding declarations to migrate them to the newmath package while preserving the behavior of existing code:
package oldmath
//go:fix inline
type Rational = newmath.Rational
//go:fix inline
const Pi = newmath.Pi
Each time the inline analyzer encounters a reference to oldmath.Rational or oldmath.Pi, it will update them to refer instead to newmath.
Under the hood of the inliner
At a glance, source inlining seems straightforward: just replace the call with the body of the callee function, introduce variables for the function parameters, and bind the call arguments to those variables. But handling all of the complexities and corner cases correctly while producing acceptable results is no small technical challenge: the inliner is about 7,000 lines of dense, compiler-like logic. Let’s look at six aspects of the problem that make it so tricky.
1. Parameter elimination
One of the inliner’s most important tasks is to attempt to replace each occurrence of a parameter in the callee by its corresponding argument from the call. In the simplest case, the argument is a trivial literal such as 0 or "", so the replacement is straightforward and the parameter can be eliminated.
//go:fix inline
func show(prefix, item string) {
    fmt.Println(prefix, item)
}
show("", "hello")
fmt.Println("", "hello")
For less trivial literals such as 404 or "go.dev", the replacement is equally straightforward, so long as the parameter appears in the callee at most once. But if it appears multiple times, it would be bad style to sprinkle copies of these magic values throughout the code as it would obscure the relationship between them; a later change to only one of them might create an inconsistency.
In such cases the inliner must tread carefully and emit a more conservative result. Whenever one or more parameters cannot be completely substituted for any reason, the inliner inserts an explicit “parameter binding” declaration:
//go:fix inline
func printPair(before, x, y, after string) {
    fmt.Println(before, x, after)
    fmt.Println(before, y, after)
}
printPair("[", "one", "two", "]")
// a “parameter binding” declaration
var before, after = "[", "]"
fmt.Println(before, "one", after)
fmt.Println(before, "two", after)
2. Side effects
In Go, as in all imperative programming languages, calling a function may have the side effect of updating variables, which in turn may affect the behavior of other functions. Consider the call to add below:
func add(x, y int) int { return y + x }
z = add(f(), g())
A trivial inlining of the call would replace x with f() and y with g(), with this result:
z = g() + f()
But this result is incorrect because evaluation of g() now occurs before f(); if the two functions have side effects, those effects will now be observed in a different order and may affect the result of the expression. Of course, it is bad form to write code that relies on effect ordering among call arguments, but that doesn’t mean people don’t do it, and our tools have to get it right.
So, the inliner must attempt to prove that f() and g() do not have side effects on each other. On success, it can safely proceed with the result above. Otherwise, it must fall back to an explicit parameter binding:
var x = f()
z = g() + x
When considering side effects, it’s not only the argument expressions that matter. Also significant is the order in which parameters are evaluated relative to other code in the callee. Consider this call to add2:
//go:fix inline
func add2(x, y int) int {
    return x + other() + y
}
add2(f(), g())
This time, parameters x and y are used in the same order they are declared, so the substitution f() + other() + g() won’t change the order of effects of f() and g()—but it will change the order of any effects of other() and g(). Furthermore, if the function body uses a parameter within a loop, substitution might change the cardinality of effects.
The inliner uses a novel hazard analysis to model the order of effects in each callee function. Nonetheless, its ability to construct the necessary safety proofs is quite limited. For example, if the calls f() and g() are simple accessors, it would be perfectly safe to call them in either order. Indeed, an optimizing compiler might use its knowledge of the internals of f and g to safely reorder the two calls. But unlike a compiler, which generates object code that reflects the source at a specific moment, the purpose of the inliner is to make permanent changes to the source, so it can’t take advantage of ephemeral details. As an extreme example, consider this start function:
func start() { /* TODO: implement */ }
An optimizing compiler is free to delete each call to start() because it has no effects today, but the inliner is not, because it may become important tomorrow.
In short, the inliner may produce results that—to the informed eye of a project maintainer—are clearly too conservative. In such cases, the fixed code would benefit stylistically from a little manual cleanup.
3. “Fallible” constant expressions
You might imagine (as I once did) that it would always be safe to replace a parameter variable by a constant argument of the same type. Surprisingly, this turns out not to be the case, because some checks previously done at run time would now happen—and fail—at compile time. Consider this call to the index function:
//go:fix inline
func index(s string, i int) byte {
    return s[i]
}
index("", 0)
A naive inliner might replace s with "" and i with 0, resulting in ""[0], but this is not actually a legal Go expression because this particular index is out of bounds for this particular string. Because the expression ""[0] is composed of constants, it is evaluated at compile time, and a program that contains it will not even build. By contrast, the original program would fail only if execution reaches this call to index, which presumably in a working program it does not.
Consequently, the inliner must keep track of all expressions and their operands that might become constant during parameter substitution, triggering additional compile-time checks. It builds a constraint system and attempts to solve it. Each unsatisfied constraint is resolved by adding an explicit binding for the constrained parameters.
4. Shadowing
Typical argument expressions contain one or more identifiers that refer to symbols (variables, functions, and so on) in the caller’s file. The inliner must make sure that each name in the argument expression would refer to the same symbol after parameter substitution; in other words, none of the caller’s names is shadowed in the callee. If this fails, the inliner must again insert parameter bindings, as in this example:
//go:fix inline
func f(val string) {
    x := 123
    fmt.Println(val, x)
}
x := "hello"
f(x)
x := "hello"
{
    // another “parameter binding” declaration
    // to read the caller's x before shadowing it
    var val string = x
    x := 123
    fmt.Println(val, x)
}
Conversely, the inliner must also check that each name in the callee function body would refer to the same thing when it is spliced into the call site. In other words, none of the callee’s names is shadowed or missing in the caller. For missing names, the inliner may need to insert additional imports.
5. Unused variables
When an argument expression has no effects and its corresponding parameter is never used, the expression may be eliminated. However, if the expression contains the last reference to a local variable at the caller, this may cause a compile error because the variable is now unused.
//go:fix inline
func f(_ int) { print("hello") }
x := 42
f(x)
x := 42 // error: unused variable: x
print("hello")
So the inliner must account for references to local variables and avoid removing the last one. (Of course it is still possible that two different inliner fixes each remove the second-to-last reference to a variable, so the two fixes are valid in isolation but not together; see the discussion of semantic conflicts in the previous post. Unfortunately manual cleanup is inevitably required in this case.)
6. Defer
In some cases, it is simply impossible to inline away the call.
Consider a call to a function that uses a defer statement:
if we were to eliminate the call, the deferred function would execute
when the caller function returns, which is too late.
All we can safely do when the callee uses defer is to
put the body of the callee in a function literal and immediately call it.
This function literal, func() { … }(), delimits the lifetime of the
defer statement, as in this example:
//go:fix inline
func callee() {
    defer f()
    …
}
callee()
func() {
    defer f()
    …
}()
If you invoke the inliner in gopls, you’ll see that it makes the change shown above and introduces the function literal. This result may be appropriate in an interactive setting, since you are likely to immediately tweak the code (or undo the fix) as you prefer, but it is rarely desirable in a batch tool, so as a matter of policy the analyzer in go fix refuses to inline such “literalized” calls.
An optimizing compiler for “tidiness”
We’ve now seen half a dozen examples of how the inliner handles tricky semantic edge cases correctly.
(Many thanks to Rob Findley, Jonathan Amsterdam, Olena Synenka, and Lasse Folger for insights, discussions, reviews, features, and fixes.)
By putting all of the smarts into the inliner, users can simply apply an “Inline call” refactoring in their IDE or add a //go:fix inline directive to their own functions and be confident that the resulting code transformations can be applied with only the most cursory review.
Although we have made good progress toward that goal, we have not yet fully attained it, and it is likely that we never will. Consider a compiler. A sound compiler produces correct output for any input and never miscompiles your code; this is the fundamental expectation that every user should have of their compiler. An optimizing compiler produces code carefully chosen for speed without compromising on safety. Similarly, an inliner is a bit like an optimizing compiler whose goal is not speed but tidiness: inlining a call must never change the behavior of your program, and ideally it produces code that is maximally neat and tidy. Unfortunately, an optimizing compiler is provably never done: showing that two different programs are equivalent is an undecidable problem, and there will always be improvements that an expert knows are safe but the compiler cannot prove. So too with the inliner: there will always be cases where the inliner’s output is too fussy or otherwise stylistically inferior to that of a human expert, and there will always be more “tidiness optimizations” to add.
Try it out!
We hope this tour of the inliner gives you a sense of some of the challenges involved, and of our priorities and directions in providing sound, self-service code transformation tools. Please try out the inliner, either interactively in your IDE, or through //go:fix inline directives and the go fix command, and share with us your experiences and any ideas you have for further improvements or new tools.


